## Architecture Impact

- architecture_relevance: relevant
- architecture_impact: medium
- architecture_reason: An externally consumed MCP discovery shape and optional read-result navigation are changed. SDK validation, Core product authority, CLI report semantics and persistent state remain owned by the existing components. A public integration-contract effect requires explicit PRD/SD treatment even though the implementation footprint is bounded.
- architecture_evidence: Main contract.js contains root allOf beside properties; main service.js returns report/presentation only. The evaluated final-candidate.patch changes those two owners and keeps navigation outside report. The current listed agdf_inspect signature still shows the collapsed conditional intersection. Schema equivalence, CLI report parity and real-agent observations exist for the isolated prototype.
- architecture_missing_evidence: none for route selection; implementation-candidate regressions, native traces and final package measurements remain future delivery obligations
- architecture_next_owner_and_action: prd-definition defines the additive behavior and acceptance; sd-definition binds the exact Core owner, advisory-failure compatibility and revision/selection rules; tp-definition binds evidence and execution budgets

## Reuse And Parallel-Structure Risk

| Finding | Evidence | Risk | Required action |
|---|---|---|---|
| problem: correct validation is not fully visible in the native Codex projection | Main contract.js root allOf; installed-host loopback traces and failed schema-only native comparison | warn | Extend the same Core field model and preserve acceptance equivalence; do not create a host-specific parallel schema owner |
| problem: callers reconstruct module and binding arguments despite existing validated owners | Main service.js; existing readPresentationCatalog and RUN_COMMAND_DESCRIPTORS; retained real input errors | warn | Derive read navigation from these owners; forbid guessed IDs, implicit target selection and a second operation map |
| problem: optional navigation must not turn a successful blocked-state read into failure or replace a conversation binding | Proposed service composition adds catalog observation to gate-check; UR CGI-03/06 | warn | PRD preserves report behavior and explicit binding precedence; SD decides failure containment and exact eligibility before TP |
| trade-off: sequence follow-up reads under the existing single-in-flight runtime | First guided prototype produced dispatch_busy during parallel reads; unchanged strict audit rejected its unsupported wrapper | warn | Keep guidance sequential in this slice; do not broaden runtime concurrency or weaken the audit to pass |
| problem: budgets leave little space for a careless transfer | Existing Copilot baseline is 397 files/3,509,461 bytes; final prototype 3,509,443 bytes; definition 4,992/5,000 bytes | warn | Reuse selector conversion and remove redundant help without losing meaning; measure normal generated payload and all current limits |
| problem: prototype evidence is not final delivery evidence | Five fresh evaluation sessions, four strict successes only on final prototype; temporary report and manifests | warn | Preserve every original observation and hash; plan fresh candidate-bound sessions; never relabel failed or earlier results |

### Retained Debt Detail

- finding: trade-off: sequence follow-up reads under the existing single-in-flight runtime
- rationale: The approved scope preserves execution and transaction boundaries. Explicit sequential read guidance addresses the observed busy failure without introducing a new concurrency architecture.
- accountable_owner: Core inspection/MCP maintainer, reviewed through sd-definition and qa-gate in this Run
- mitigation: Explicit guidance, purely advisory read calls, actual STDIO checks, negative binding cases and fresh native traces with no busy result; unchanged audit and approval predicates
- review_date_or_exit_condition: Reassess at this Run's QA before UAT. A remaining busy failure in the approved normal scenario prevents pass; any need for new concurrency behavior returns to SD and scope review instead of being silently accepted.

